Z||000011||Wannabe||09-08-2001||10:46 AM||||You will have a hard time finding any 15 year old car that won't need work on a regular basis.||||reg||1
Z||000012||Joe Torma||09-08-2001||10:58 AM||||The 3rg Gen have lots of problems...just wait till you spend like $300 on a Mass Air Flow sensor.  My friend's GTA digidash doesn't work right, and it runs 15.7(g-tech) what'ts the Fiero run?  My brother's 305(worked up) does 0-60 in like 7(.?) seconds.  Yuk.<P>They are cool though...you can find them anywhere(read:every joker has owned one, and rigged something on it to get it to run)<P>Now, if my Fiero book was done(where are your pics people) you'd have some inspiration to fix your car.<P>Like we're not here to help?!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>------------------<BR>   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/simplesig.gif">  <BR>Did you get your car in my book yet????<BR> <A HREF="http://www.animatedconcepts.com/fierobook.htm" TARGET=_blank>http://www.animatedconcepts.com/fierobook.htm</A> ||||reg||1
Z||000013||Spektyr||09-08-2001||12:32 PM||||My first car was a 2.8L 1981 Chevy Citation.  Virtually the same motor as the Fiero, only not fuel injected.<P>It was entirely too damn fast.  (Auto tranny shifted from 2nd to 3rd at about 90mph.)<P>It was also incredibly reliable... You could count on it to break down at LEAST once a month.  If you hit a really big bump, the motor would die and give you hell trying to restart it.<P>And I had a damn fun time driving it.  (learned a lot about fixing cars, too.)<P>However, no one looked twice at my Citation unless I had it floored (because it smoked really bad).<P>When people come over and see my non-running, sick Fiero in the garage, they seem compelled to go over and look at it.<P>You just have to pay attention to Fiero's.  They're like women -- constant work to keep them happy.  If you're alert you'll see problems before she blows up at you and leaves you cold and stranded.  And like a woman she won't hold up a neon sign saying "Dude!  There's a problem!" (although Fiero's do at least have the 'Check Engine' light.)<P>But yet again, like a woman, if you take care of her, she'll do amazing things for you and turn all your buddies green with envy.  Even better, she doesn't get jealous if you stop to pick up another woman (or even another Fiero!).<P>------------------<BR>85 GT (to be modified...)<BR>--Um, no the paint isn't oxidized, that's the ultra-rare Whirlpool White textured finish... yeah.<BR>89 Lincoln Town Car (has Fiero Envy)<BR>--Hey!  Can I have an anti-sway bar too?||||reg||1
Z||000014||Shiner||09-08-2001||06:17 PM||||I'm sorry, but those 305 SBCs were 'efin pigs, especially with an auto.  I don't think trading a late 80's GM car for another late 80's GM car is going to have any effect on the relaibility.  All 80's GM crap breaks down all the time.||||reg||1
Z||000015||rogergarrison||09-08-2001||07:50 PM||||I just fix anything that messes up when it breaks. Dont let it go till you have 20 things to fix. Ive had very few (minor at that) problems at all in 87K and I run it fast, not pampered. My biggest problem was the Pontiac dealer machanics who blew the clutch out a month after I got it on the road. Of course they wouldnt fess up, worked perfect when I dropped it off, would barely move when I picked it up. Id fill up the tank and leave for California rite now and not have a worry. Only car Ive had out of dozens and dozens, except Lincolns, Ive had no trouble with.||||reg||1
